n  I ran and competed in that election as a  third party candidate not as a Democrat  not as a Republican I had no money and I  was affiliated with a very small  progressive third party called the  Liberty Union party 1971  and I thought I had no illusions that I  was gonna win this election but I  thought it would be important to go out  around the state and talk to people  expressing my views about the state of  the economy the Vietnam War as was then  going on and see how we could do well on  Election Day the votes came in and I  received not thirty percent of the vote  not forty percent of the vote but two  percent of the vote  so the first point that I'm gonna make  to you today is don't give up but I was  not smart enough unlike many of you I  was not very smart and I said hey I got  two percent of the vote I'm gonna try  again and in Vermont which i think is a  good thing we have elections every two  years for statewide offices and so forth  so another year came in 1972 and I said  I am gonna run for governor of the state  of Vermont having won overwhelming  support with 2% what do I have to lose  how much worse can I do well I actually  did worse I went from 2% down to 1% but  the voter turnout was larger so I got  more actual votes but my percentage went  down again being one of those people  who's too dumb to know when he's licked  you know I came back again and two years  later I ran for the United States Senate  again and instead of getting 2% of the  vote I got my total all the way up to 4%  two years later I ran for governor of  the state of Vermont and this time I  made it up to 6 percent for races and I  managed to fight my way up to 6 upset  now see some of you are smiling but what  you should know if you check your  history running is a third party  candidate actually getting six percent  of the vote is not bad it is very hard  because people in our country  traditionally they're a Democrat they're  gonna vote Republican asking them to go  outside 